# Mascot starter decks

These two additional Modern practice starters keep Pikachu and Eevee at the centre of their themes. They are available as borrowed battle decks and editable starter templates, including Random selection. Existing starters remain available.

## Pika Power

14 Pokémon, 32 Trainers, 14 Basic Lightning Energy.

- 3 charging Pikachu (`swsh1-65`) and 1 Wild Charge Pikachu (`swsh12pt5-160`).
- 1 Raichu with Electrocharge and 200-damage Thunderbolt (`sv2-64`), plus 3 Raichu with Strong Volt (`me2pt5-56`). This is the tested 2026-10-05 balance split.
- 2 Dedenne (`sv8-87`) recover discarded Trainers using the Colorless-cost Electromagnetic Sonar; the deck does not supply Psychic Energy for Gnaw.
- 2 Bidoof and 2 Bibarel support drawing cards.

Use Charge or Electrocharge to prepare Raichu. Thunderbolt discards its Energy, so Energy Retrieval returns Basic Energy to hand and Super Rod returns Pokémon and Energy to the deck. Switch and Air Balloon help rotate attackers. Energy Retrieval requires Basic Energy in your own discard pile.

## Eevee’s Rainbow

14 Pokémon, 31 Trainers, 15 Energy (4 Fire, 4 Water, 4 Lightning, 3 Double Turbo).

- 2 Eevee with Signs of Evolution (`swsh45-52`) find an evolution.
- 2 Eevee with Colorful Catch (`sv6pt5-50`) find Basic Energy of different types.
- 2 each of Vaporeon (`sv8pt5-22`), Jolteon (`sv8pt5-29`) and Flareon (`sv8pt5-13`).
- 2 Bidoof and 2 Bibarel support drawing cards.

Choose an evolution based on the available Energy and opposing Pokémon. Vaporeon can heal, Jolteon can target the Bench, and Flareon can discard opposing Energy on a coin flip. Each has a stronger attack against Pokémon ex and V. Double Turbo pays the two Colorless requirements but reduces attack damage by 20. Super Rod recycles the four Eevee, which share the four-copy name limit across their two printings.

The picker uses existing card artwork: Pikachu or Eevee on the featured stack, with the supporting Pokémon fanned behind it. These fan decks do not require a Legendary. Their printings are active and implemented in the game; the pool's implemented-unreviewed label still applies.


