## Validation and availability

The ten additions are discovered through `ShowdownDecks.All()` by starter templates, borrowed solo decks, the multiplayer starter picker and Random selection. Each uses its Legendary card artwork with three supporting-card previews.

The structural audit checks 60 cards, name-copy limits, active playable printings, complete evolution lines, supplied attack Energy types and actual preview artwork. The shared Legendary scenario verifies one featured Legendary copy per themed deck and that its attacks can be paid for.

An initial 1,200 Hard-AI matches (40 against each of two existing decks and one neighboring new theme per addition) completed without errors or timeouts. Abyssal Tide was then revised to include two Wailord V; another 300 matches of that final list completed cleanly. Its wins were 55/100 against Burn Bright V2, 30/100 against Ice Current V2 and 21/100 against Thunder Safari. These small AI batches check playability and flag consistency issues; they do not establish equal balance or human win rates. Initial match snapshots preserve the earlier list.

Evidence and final deck lists are under `reports/legendary-decks/`. Card definitions were unchanged, and the content import preserved compatible prior match hashes.

The Windows build passed all 5,384 native verification scenarios. Final picker checks passed 1,073 checks each at 1672×941 and 1100×800, with no text overflow. They verify all 22 exact starter copies, paging, Random, Undo, persistence and battle selection. The test now removes disposable starter copies before later fixtures, avoiding the 30-saved-deck limit as the roster grows. The final rebuild reused the byte-verified native evidence after this UI-test-only fix. Run `Play-Unity.ps1` for the updated `builds/ModernWindows/PokemonCardTable.exe`.

## Approved balance update — 5 October 2026

The approved Abyssal Tide, Enchanted Grove, Stormwing Squadron and Phoenix Rising lists now match the simulation-only recommendation exactly. The other 13 starters retain their existing lists. The game descriptions and starter previews reflect the new cards.

The combined proposal completed 13,600 Hard-AI games (100 per pairing across all 17 decks), with one draw, zero errors and zero timeouts. Mean win rates were 45.4%, 50.4%, 53.4% and 54.6%, respectively. These results estimate play under the implemented AI and rules; they are not guaranteed human win rates. Earlier validation above describes historical lists and builds.

The exact approved lists, before/after heat matrix and 90% intervals remain in [the proposal report](../reports/simulations/four-deck-balance-proposal-100-20261005/matrix.html). Build and application evidence is recorded in `reports/approved-four-deck-balance-20261005/`.


